Backport of upstream commit 692439ec8d41008f9d9726359013f44d8473ad7b
("monitor/wayland: Don't leak zxdg_output_v1 proxy"), which landed on the
gtk-3-24 branch after 3.24.52 and is therefore missing from the tarball
nixpkgs currently builds.

gdk_wayland_monitor_finalize() destroys the monitor's wl_output but never the
zxdg_output_v1 proxy created for it by
gdk_wayland_screen_get_xdg_output(). The proxy keeps a listener whose `data`
is the monitor itself, so once the monitor is freed the compositor can still
deliver an xdg_output.name event to a dangling pointer:

  xdg_output_handle_name()
    g_free (monitor->name);          <-- reads freed memory

This is reachable on any output hotplug. A compositor that repeatedly
re-adds and removes a connector turns it into a reliable crash: every
GTK3 client in the session receives the output removal plus a fresh
xdg_output.name event per cycle, and they all die together.

Observed on this machine with niri and a display whose internal USB hub
re-enumerates every ~10s, dropping and restoring the DP hotplug line on
each cycle (2792 times in one boot). Seven GTK3 applications crashed
within 16 seconds of each other:

  chrome, electron, code, thunar-volman, QQ, Telegram, iMe

All shared the same stack:

  #0  <client>
  #1  g_free (libglib-2.0.so.0)
  #2  xdg_output_handle_name (libgdk-3.so.0)

GTK4 already does the right thing; gdkmonitor-wayland.c there calls
g_clear_pointer (&monitor->xdg_output, zxdg_output_v1_destroy) before
releasing the wl_output.

--- a/gdk/wayland/gdkmonitor-wayland.c
+++ b/gdk/wayland/gdkmonitor-wayland.c
@@ -38,6 +38,7 @@ gdk_wayland_monitor_finalize (GObject *object)
   g_free (monitor->name);
 
   wl_output_destroy (monitor->output);
+  g_clear_pointer (&monitor->xdg_output, zxdg_output_v1_destroy);
 
   G_OBJECT_CLASS (gdk_wayland_monitor_parent_class)->finalize (object);
 }
